            <ArticleTitle>Immediate ureterovaginal fistula secondary to oocyte retrieval--a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2006.e1-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.fertnstert.2008.03.005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report a case of ureterovaginal fistula secondary to transvaginal oocyte retrieval (TVOR).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">IVF Center IN a private hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 31-year-old woman presented immediately after TVOR with right lower abdominal pain with irradiation to the suprapubic area and vaginal discharge.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Vaginal examination, creatinine dosage in plasma and vaginal discharge, excretory urography. A double-J catheter was inserted under general anesthesia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Clinical follow-up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT(S)" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Vaginal leakage ceased a few hours after catheter insertion. Transfer of two embryos was performed 3 days after TVOR, but no pregnancy occurred. The double-J catheter was removed 21 days after its placement. Imaging studies done 6 weeks later demonstrated a normal urinary tract morphology.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION(S)" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Given the elective nature of TVOR and IVF, patients should be informed about all potential complications, including ureterovaginal fistula.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
